## DELEGATION GUIDANCE (context isolation first)

The primary reason to use sub-agents is **context isolation** — keep
exploration, search output, and verification logs out of your own context so
this session can stay focused on the work units and proof requirements. Speed
and breadth are secondary benefits, not the goal. Sub-agents do not replace
fresh-context analysis phases; they speed up the work that feeds them.

### When delegation is permitted

Use sub-agents only when one of the following concrete triggers is true:

- Independent repository discovery that would otherwise flood this context
  (many files, large directories, broad web research that can be summarized
  compactly on return).
- Independent verification checks that return compact evidence (a single
  command plus exit code, or a small set of file:line citations) without
  polluting the main session with the command's stdout.
- Ready work units the plan already declared under `## Work Units`.

A vague "this might help" is not a trigger. A single-file read, a quick
search, or a focused pytest run is not worth the coordination cost — do it
in this session.

### Concrete verification fan-out example

When the change set is large enough that the focused checks collectively
flood this session, dispatch read-only verification sub-agents, one per
criterion group, while you continue with implementation. The four concrete
groups are: **build** (compilation, packaging, install), **lint** (style and
lint gates), **types** (static type checks), and **focused tests** (the
per-step `Verify` commands, parametrized to a tight scope). Each subagent
returns the same four-field shape per check:

- **Expected** — the observation that would mean the check passed.
- **Observed** — the literal command output or file:line citation the
  subagent read.
- **Evidence** — the exact command plus its exit code, or `path:line` for
  a static read.
- **Location** — the file, line, or step ID the evidence attaches to.

Every subagent lead a verdict depends on — passing or failing — is
reproduced in this session (re-run the command, or re-read the cited
`path:line`) before the verdict is written, matching the "Reading
sub-agent output" rule below.

